4,7,4,9,9,4],[4,6,4,4,4,6,4,7,4,4,4,4],[4,6,6,6,6,6,4,7,4,9,9,4],[0,4,4,4,4,4,4,7,4,4,4,4],[0,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,4,9,9,4],[0,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4],[4,4,0,0,4,4,0,4,4,4,4,4]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"The earlier program used only one marker line to choose a rotation, and that was the bug. A single side-to-side constraint leaves two candidates, a rotation and a reflection, and it can pick the wrong one.\n\nThe two marker lines sit on adjacent sides of the rectangle. Requiring both to land on their matching edges fixes exactly one of the 8 rotations and reflections.\n\n**Correction for pair 1.** The 4-line is on the right and must go to the top. The 6-line is on the bottom and must go to the left. A counter-clockwise rotation sends right→top but bottom→right, so it is wrong. The transform that satisfies both is the anti-transpose, which is a reflection. The verifier's mismatch shows exactly this: the expected output is the predicted one mirrored left-right.\n\n**Pairs 2 and 3.** Both mappings there match a counter-clockwise rotation, which is why those pairs were already exact.\n\n**Finding the transform.** For each of the 8 candidates (`rot90(A,k)` and `rot90(fliplr(A),k)`), apply it to a 3×3 tag grid. The tag grid has a code written in the middle of each side. Read back where each side code ended up, and keep the candidate that sends every marker side to its edge side.\n\n**Test input.** The 2-marker is on the left and must go to the top. The 3-marker is on the bottom and must go to the right. The program picks whichever of the 8 transforms satisfies both, and the result is a 20×12 grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    bg = background(g)\n    comps = components(g != bg)\n    inner = np.zeros_like(g, dtype=bool)\n    for c in comps:\n        if not any(y in (0, H-1) or x in (0, W-1) for y, x in c):\n            for y, x in c:\n                inner[y, x] = True\n    lines = [g[0, :], g[:, W-1], g[H-1, :], g[:, 0]]\n    edge_col = {}\n    for s, ln in enumerate(lines):\n        vals = [int(v) for v in ln if v != bg]\n        if vals:\n            c = Counter(vals).most_common(1)[0][0]\n            edge_col.setdefault(c, s)\n    rect_cells = [(y, x) for y, x in zip(*np.nonzero(inner)) if int(g[y, x]) not in edge_col]\n    ry0, rx0, ry1, rx1 = bbox(rect_cells)\n    crop_ = crop(g, ry0, rx0, ry1, rx1)\n    emit(crop_, \"Crop the rectangle (inside its marker lines) out of the grid.\")\n    # constraints: marker side -> edge side\n    cons = []\n    for c, es in edge_col.items():\n        cells = [(y, x) for y, x in zip(*np.nonzero(inner)) if g[y, x] == c]\n        if not cells:\n            continue\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = bbox(cells)\n        if y1 < ry0:\n            side = 0\n        elif y0 > ry1:\n            side = 2\n        elif x1 < rx0:\n            side = 3\n        else:\n            side = 1\n        cons.append((side, es))\n    pos = {0: (0, 1), 1: (1, 2), 2: (2, 1), 3: (1, 0)}\n    tag = np.zeros((3, 3), dtype=int)\n    for s, (y, x) in pos.items():\n        tag[y, x] = s + 1\n    cands = []\n    for f in (False, True):\n        for k in range(4):\n            cands.append((f, k))\n    best = None\n    for f, k in cands:\n        t = np.rot90(np.fliplr(tag) if f else tag, k)\n        ok = True\n        for side, es in cons:\n            y, x = pos[es]\n            if t[y, x] != side + 1:\n                ok = False\n                break\n        if ok:\n            best = (f, k)\n            break\n    if best is None:\n        best = (False, 0)\n    f, k = best\n    out = np.rot90(np.fliplr(crop_) if f else crop_, k)\n    emit(out, f\"Orient the crop (flip={f}, {k} CCW quarter-turns) so each marker line faces the grid edge of its colour.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"rigid":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The earlier program used only one marker line to choose a rotation, and that was the bug."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"A single side-to-side constraint leaves two candidates, a rotation and a reflection, and it can pick the wrong one."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Requiring both to land on their matching edges fixes exactly one of the 8 rotations and reflections."}],"crop":[{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Crop the rectangle (inside its marker lines) out of the grid."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Orient the crop (flip=True, 3 CCW quarter-turns) so each marker line faces the grid edge of its colour."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Orient the crop (flip=False, 1 CCW quarter-turns) so each marker line faces the grid edge of its colour."}],"assembly":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"A single side-to-side constraint leaves two candidates, a rotation and a reflection, and it can pick the wrong one."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"aa8be9889ec260fc7d9ad2d8fce737c61e9395c74a49044b3319934a081b04e7","task_sha256":"9aff062a2795cffa15ff120566b385187455dee49ca3013ef6c2cde84c3b158e","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
